## Hoechst/PI staining
For the Hoechst/PI staining, cells were treated with RPMI $1640+\mathrm{B}27-$ supplemented with $12\upmu\mathrm{M}$ CHIR with or without BI-1347 for $^{48\mathrm{h},}$ then were incubated with RPMI $1640+\mathrm{B}27-$ supplemented with $5\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ Hoechst 33342 (Yeasen), maintained at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . $100\times$ PI stain Solution (Coolaber, SL7091) was diluted with PBS and cells were incubated with diluted PI for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. Cells were immediately imaged using Celldiscoverer 7.
RNA was extracted using the EasyPure $\textcircled{8}$ RNA Kit (TransGen), then sequenced on NovaSeq6000-PE150. The reads were processed and mapped to the Homo Sapiens genome $\mathrm{GRCh38/hg38}$ . The figures of PCA, heatmaps, and GO enrichment were visualized by Omicshare Tools (https://www.omicshare.com/). Heatmaps represent $\log_{2}(\mathrm{FPKM}+1)$ normalized over samples. The detection of the differentially expressed genes (DEG calling) between samples was performed with DESeq92 when samples included multiple repetitions. Only $P$ value $<0.05$ , fold change $<0.5$ or $>2$ , and mean expression $>1$ were regarded as DEGs and used in further analysis. DEG calling was performed with expression fold change in samples without repetition. Only fold change $<0.5$ or $>2$ , and mean expression $>1$ were regarded as DEGs. The front-rank DEGs were enriched for GO analysis.
More details can be accessed through GEO accession: GSE226159.
## Image acquisition
The live-cell bright-field images within the whole CM differentiation process were acquired using Celldiscoverer 7 (an imaging system equipped with an inverted research microscope for long-term cell culture observations, made by Carl Zeiss). The ORCA-Flash $4.0\ \mathrm{V}3$ digital CMOS camera with $2048\times2048$ pixels could achieve automatic live-cell imaging. During differentiation, the petri dish was maintained in an internal incubator inside the microscope at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%\mathrm{CO_{2}}$ .
The 96-well, 24-well, and 384-well plates (Falcon) were used. After autofocus, bright-field images were taken under a $5\times$ objective and a $2\times$ tube lens. A $2\times2$ binning was applied to each acquisition to improve the signal-tonoise ratio and reduce the storage space, thus generating $1024\times1024$ -pixel images. The scaling resolution was $1.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ per pixel according to the previous light path design.
Each well in the 96-well plate was imaged with 25 tiles in a $5\times5$ pattern with an overlap of $5\%-15\%$ on adjacent fields. After stitching, the tiles of each well generated an image with $4860\times4860$ pixels, $6.3\:\mathrm{mm}\times6.3\:\mathrm{mm}$ in size. To ensure the sharpness of the images, $3{-}5\ Z$ -stacks of images with equidistant intervals $(3-6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ were used for further in-focus image selection. For three typical Z-stacks of images, a whole 96-well plate scanning takes ${\sim}1.2\mathrm{h}$ for 7200 $(5\times5\times3\times96)$ tiles in one round. For the following analysis, only images of 9 tiles in a $3\times3$ pattern located in the center of the well were used as “whole-well images” to avoid the interference of images containing well edges.
For the 24-well plate, similarly, 156 tiles were stitched into a $20,284\times20,284$ -pixel rectangle image for each well. In particular, for some wells at the edge of the plate, only 136 tiles were taken because of the limitation of the shooting range of the objective lens. An approximate $13.0\mathrm{mm}\times13.0\mathrm{mm}$ visual range was obtained for each well. A total of 10,992 $(136\times3\times4+156\times3\times20)$ images were collected in one round.
For the 384-well plate (square well), 9 tiles in a $3\times3$ pattern with an overlap of $10\%$ were taken. The scaling resolution was $1.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ per pixel with the same objective and camera. In total, 3456 $(3\times3\times1\times384)$ ) images were collected for image acquisition in one round.
The cell images were automatically saved in CZI format generated by ZEN software (Zeiss company supporting picture acquisition software, version V2.0-V3.1 was used). Images were automatically exported to uncompressed TIFF format or PNG format for real-time image processing.
## Visualization of the local image features of the overall differentiation process
The local image features of the overall CM differentiation process were first analyzed by PCA and LDA42. The dataset consisted of $n=19,968$ images (for 96 wells at 208 time points). The feature vector for each image was obtained from $\operatorname{SIFT}^{39}$ , $\mathrm{SURF}^{40}$ , and $\mathrm{ORB}^{41}$ feature descriptors. SIFT, SURF, and ORB was first used to extract keypoints from the image and compute a feature vector (of 128-D, 64-D, and 32-D, respectively) for each keypoint; then for every image, the mean and standard deviation of each dimension of all the keypoints’ feature vector were computed, resulting in a $(128+64+32)\times2=448–\mathrm{D}$ vector. PCA was applied to project the feature vectors to the 2-D plane spanned by the first two principal components (denoted by PC1 and PC2). LDA was applied to find a 2-D plane (spanned by the first two linear discriminants, LD1 and LD2) which can best separate wells with an underdose, optimal dose, and overdose of CHIR. Extraction of local features, PCA, and LDA were implemented using the OpenCV93 and the scikit-learn package94 in Python.
## Evaluation of differentiation efficiency using cTnT fluorescent labels
The PSC-to-CM differentiation efficiency of a well was quantified by the average intensities of the final fluorescence images. Concretely, for a $W\times W$ fluorescence image $I$ (with intensity values in [0, 1]), its “Differentiation Efficiency Index” was defined by the total intensity values greater than a threshold $\alpha$ , i.e.,
$$
\mathrm{DifferentiationEfficiencyIndex}=\frac{1}{W^{2}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq i,j\leq W,}}I_{i,j},
$$
where $1/W^{2}$ is a normalizing factor. In all of our experiments, $\alpha$ was chosen to be 0.5.
